WEBVTT

00:00:00.009 --> 00:00:04.667
When a record is questionable, one useful strategy is quarantine.

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:10.412
Quarantine does not necessarily mean moving the record to another physical database table.

00:00:10.412 --> 00:00:18.642
The main idea is to separate questionable data from trusted analytical data while preserving the source.

00:00:18.642 --> 00:00:21.632
First, keep the original record.

00:00:21.632 --> 00:00:27.018
Second, create issue flags that describe what is missing, invalid, or suspicious.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:33.047
Third, create a review subset containing only records that need attention.

00:00:33.047 --> 00:00:41.812
Later, if evidence becomes available, those records can be corrected, approved, or promoted to a trusted layer.

00:00:41.812 --> 00:00:48.237
For example, an invalid signup date should not be silently replaced with today's date.

00:00:48.237 --> 00:00:55.527
We can preserve the raw date text, produce a null cleaned date, and flag the record for review.

00:00:55.527 --> 00:01:01.193
Quarantine makes uncertainty visible instead of pretending the problem has been solved.
